[1mNAME[0m
       roffsub - a roff subset for manuals read in a terminal

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       A roffsub document is a man page. Every roff program displays it: man, nroff, groff, mandoc. It is also simple
       enough for small awk and C routines to render from inside a program's own binary or bash function, at the width
       of the terminal in use, with bold and italic where the terminal can show them. One source then serves as the
       installed page and as the program's built-in help.

       This page defines the source form, how it is rendered, and how a program presents it. The form is checked;
       content is not.

[1mELEMENTS[0m
        [1mELEMENT[0m         [1mWRITTEN[0m [1mAS[0m                  [1mSHOWN[0m [1mAS[0m
       ---------------+---------------------------+-------------------------------------
        title           .TH                         header and footer, optional in help
        section         .SH                         bold heading at the margin
        subsection      .SS                         bold heading at half an indent
        paragraph       .PP .P .LP                  filled text
        definition      .TP, tag on the next line   tag, body hanging at the indent
        list item       .IP                         as a definition, tag inline
        nested block    .RS .RE                     margin moved in and back
        literal block   .nf .fi                     lines as written
        emphasis        font escapes and requests   bold literals, italic arguments
        spacing         .sp .br                     blank lines, a line break
        table           .TS .TE                     open columns, horizontal rules
        comment         .\"                         nothing

[1mSOURCE FORM[0m
       Each rule states its purpose, so it can be judged by whether it achieves it.

   [1mLexical rules[0m
       o  Bytes 0x20 to 0x7E and newline only. A byte is then a column, so awk and C measure width with a plain length.

       o  No tab characters. Layout is stated by requests, so every viewer shows the same thing, and tab is free to
          separate fields in the compiled form.

       o  Line 1 is [1m'\"[0m [1mt[0m when, and only when, the page has a table, so that man runs tbl first. It is the only line
          that begins with an apostrophe.

       o  A control line is a period followed directly by a request listed below. A comment is a whole line beginning
          with a period, a backslash, and a double quote.

       o  In fill mode a text line begins with a word: never a space, never empty, and never a period or apostrophe
          unless written after \&. roff treats a leading space or a blank line as a layout instruction; stating breaks
          with requests gives them one meaning everywhere.

       o  One sentence per line is recommended. Diffs stay small and sentence spacing stays predictable.

   [1mRequests[0m
       Widths are a bare number or a number followed by n, counting character cells.

        [1mREQUEST[0m                   [1mARGUMENTS[0m                     [1mMEANING[0m
       -------------------------+-----------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------
        .TH                       NAME SEC DATE SOURCE MANUAL   page title; first request; @DATE@ and @VERSION@ are
                                                                filled in at install and compile
        .SH .SS                   heading                       section, subsection
        .PP .P .LP                none                          new paragraph; resets the prevailing indent
        .TP                       [width]                       tagged paragraph; the next line, text or a font
                                                                request, is the tag
        .IP                       [tag [width]]                 indented paragraph, optionally tagged
        .RS                       [width]                       move the margin in by width or by the prevailing indent
        .RE                       none                          restore the margin
        .nf .fi                   none                          literal and fill mode; literal blocks hold text only
        .B .I                     words                         bold or italic words
        .BR .RB .IR .RI .BI .IB   words                         two fonts alternating word by word
        .sp                       [N]                           N blank lines, default 1
        .br                       none                          line break
        .TS .TE                   none                          table

       Double quotes group words into one argument; a quote inside is written \(dq. A request takes at most 9 arguments.

   [1mEscapes[0m
        [1mESCAPE[0m        [1mMEANING[0m                          [1mTERMINAL[0m [1mOUTPUT[0m
       -------------+--------------------------------+-----------------
        \fB \fI \fR   bold, italic, roman              font change
        \fP           previous font                    font change
        \-            minus, copies as a real hyphen   -
        \e            backslash                        \
        \&            zero-width guard                 nothing
        \(space)      space that never breaks          space
        \(em          em dash                          --
        \(en          en dash                          -
        \(bu          bullet                           o
        \(aq          apostrophe                       '
        \(dq          double quote                     "

       The font returns to roman by the end of every request line and table cell, so emphasis never runs past where it
       was meant to stop.

   [1mTables[0m
       .TS
       box tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       OPTION@MEANING
       _
       \fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
       .TE

       The options line is required and ends with a semicolon. It names the cell separator with tab(c), a printable
       character that is not a letter or digit and appears in no cell, and may add box, allbox, and center. Format lines
       follow, the last ending with a period; keys are l, r, and c, with modifiers b, i, and w(N), a minimum width.
       Format line i governs data row i and the last repeats. Each data row has one cell per column; a row of a single
       underscore is a rule. A cell opened with T{ continues on the following lines until a line beginning T}, and is
       filled within its column. roff wraps only these text blocks, so any cell that may need wrapping is written as
       one; the roffsub renderers wrap every cell. The page installed for man is written by roffsub -n, which rewrites
       each table so that roff draws it the same way: an empty separator column between the columns, rules joined by a +
       in those columns, no vertical lines, and columns holding text blocks widened to fill the line.

   [1mContent[0m
       A conventional order is NAME as [4mname[0m \- [4msummary[0m, then SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, OPTIONS, EXAMPLES, ENVIRONMENT,
       FILES, EXIT STATUS, SEE ALSO. The order is guidance; content belongs to the author.

[1mRENDERING[0m

   [1mWidth and margins[0m
       Text fills to the width with a right margin of 0, or to the measure when one is set. Words are packed greedily
       with a ragged right edge; a word too long for its line runs past the margin whole. Literal lines are never
       rewrapped. The indent unit is 7 cells, or 4 when the width is under 60.

   [1mIndentation and spacing[0m
       o  A section heading sits at the margin and its body one unit in; a subsection heading sits half a unit in.

       o  Each paragraph, tagged paragraph, and indented paragraph is preceded by one blank line, except directly after
          a heading.

       o  A tag shorter than the prevailing indent shares its line with the body; a tag as long or longer puts the body
          on the next line.

       o  A width given to .TP or .IP becomes the prevailing indent for later items until the next .SH, .SS, or .PP.

       o  .RS saves the margin and prevailing indent, moves the margin in, and resets the prevailing indent; .RE
          restores both.

   [1mTables[0m
       Tables draw no vertical lines. Each cell has one space of padding on each side and columns meet at one separator
       position, a space in data rows and a plus sign in rules, so column text sits three cells apart. Rules appear
       where the source has an underscore row; box adds one above and below, allbox one after every row.

       When a table is wider than the space available, the widest columns give up width first, down to a common cap,
       never below their longest word, and short columns keep their natural width. Cells wrap within their columns. When
       even the longest words do not fit, each row is shown as lines of the form [4mheader[0m: [4mvalue[0m.

   [1mEmphasis[0m
        [1mSTYLE[0m        [1mUSED[0m [1mFOR[0m                    [1mBOLD[0m     [1mITALIC[0m
       ------------+---------------------------+--------+-------------------
        sgr          a terminal                  ESC[1m   ESC[4m, underline
        overstrike   a pager                     c BS c   _ BS c
        plain        a file, a pipe, TERM=dumb   none     none
        pre          pre-formatting              \fB      \fI

       Control bytes are added only as a line is written, so they never count toward its width. Emphasis is closed
       before every newline and reopened on the next line. The SGR parameters may be replaced, so that bold and italic
       show as colors.

[1mPROGRAM INTERFACE[0m

   [1mHelp[0m
       [1m--help[0m presents the full manual. What it writes is fixed by four inputs: the embedded manual, the width, the
       style, and the destination. The C and bash forms of a program write the same bytes for the same inputs.

        [1mOUTPUT[0m                   [1mGOES[0m [1mTO[0m            [1mSTYLE[0m        [1mWIDTH[0m
       ------------------------+------------------+------------+----------
        terminal                 the pager          overstrike   terminal
        terminal, PAGER is cat   the terminal       sgr          terminal
        file or pipe             the file or pipe   plain        80

       The pager is PAGER when set, otherwise less, otherwise more. The width is MANWIDTH when set, otherwise COLUMNS,
       otherwise the terminal's own. ROFFSUB_STYLE, ROFFSUB_SGR, and ROFFSUB_MEASURE override the style, the emphasis
       parameters, and the measure; TERM=dumb selects plain. ROFFSUB_HEADING moves section headings that many columns in
       from the margin, leaving everything else in place.

   [1mUsage[0m
       [1m-h[0m serves a different purpose from the manual: a quick reference, a refresher and confirmation when a full review
       is not needed. It is composed for that purpose, not extracted from the manual.

   [1mProfiles[0m
       A man page is built from the source and installed in every case. Inside the program the manual is carried in one
       of three profiles.

        [1mPROFILE[0m        [1mTHE[0m [1mPROGRAM[0m [1mCARRIES[0m                                   [1mHELP[0m [1mGIVES[0m
       --------------+-----------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------
        full           records and the renderer                              any width, emphasis by terminal
        preformatted   text set at 80 columns, fonts kept, and the emitter   fixed width, emphasis by terminal
        plain          text set at 80 columns                                fixed width

       The full profile is used when the renderer is no larger than the program it would live in, not counting the
       manual, and an exported bash function stays within its size budget; otherwise preformatted. Plain is used only
       when a program asks for it.

[1mCOMPILED FORM[0m
       One record per line: an operation, then fields separated by tabs. The operations are th, sh, ss, pp, tp, ip, rs,
       re, nf, fi, tx, sp, br, and for tables ts, tf, tr, t_, and te; the first record is v 1. Text fields carry only
       \fB, \fI, \fR, \\, and \~, and end in roman. Records are passed to awk on its standard input, never with -v,
       which would rewrite their backslashes.

[1mCONFORMANCE[0m
        [1mID[0m    [1mRULE[0m
       -----+---------------------------------------------------------
        E01   byte outside 0x20 to 0x7E
        E02   request not in roffsub, or an apostrophe control line
        E03   escape not in roffsub
        E04   unbalanced .nf/.fi, .RS/.RE, .TS/.TE, or T{/T}
        E05   fill-mode text line empty or beginning with a space
        E06   font not roman at a request line or cell end
        E07   the table line and the tables disagree
        E08   table form
        E09   .TH missing or not the first request
        E10   request arguments
        E11   .TP without a tag line
        W01   option hyphen written without \-
        W02   abbreviation ending a line without \&
        W03   table wider than 71 columns with no text blocks to wrap

[1mEXAMPLES[0m
       Each example shows its source, then the same source rendered in place.

   [1mOpen table with a header rule[0m
       .TS
       tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       OPTION@MEANING
       _
       \fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
       \fB\-k\fR \fIkey\fR@sort on \fIkey\fR
       .TE

        [1mOPTION[0m   [1mMEANING[0m
       --------+-------------------
        [1m-r[0m       reverse the order
        [1m-k[0m [4mkey[0m   sort on [4mkey[0m

   [1mBoxed table, aligned columns[0m
       The box option adds rules above and below; keys r and c align right and center.

       .TS
       box tab(@);
       lb cb rb
       l  c  r.
       NAME@KIND@SIZE
       _
       alpha@file@12
       beta@directory@3456
       .TE

       -------+-----------+------
        [1mNAME[0m      [1mKIND[0m      [1mSIZE[0m
       -------+-----------+------
        alpha     file        12
        beta    directory   3456
       -------+-----------+------

   [1mRuled table, centered[0m
       The allbox option puts a rule after every row; center places the table in the middle of the line.

       .TS
       allbox center tab(@);
       cb cb
       r  l.
       CODE@MEANING
       0@success
       1@a check found errors
       2@a usage or input error
       .TE

                                                ------+------------------------
                                                 [1mCODE[0m          [1mMEANING[0m
                                                ------+------------------------
                                                    0   success
                                                ------+------------------------
                                                    1   a check found errors
                                                ------+------------------------
                                                    2   a usage or input error
                                                ------+------------------------

   [1mText blocks that wrap[0m
       A cell written between T{ and T} wraps within its column, in roff and in the roffsub renderers alike.

       .TS
       tab(@);
       lb lb
       l  l.
       TERM@DEFINITION
       _
       measure@T{
       the longest line that filled text may reach,
       whatever the width of the terminal
       T}
       prevailing indent@T{
       the indent that tagged and indented paragraphs use
       when they give none of their own
       T}
       .TE

        [1mTERM[0m                [1mDEFINITION[0m
       -------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        measure             the longest line that filled text may reach, whatever the width of the terminal
        prevailing indent   the indent that tagged and indented paragraphs use when they give none of their own

   [1mMinimum column width[0m
       A key with w(N) keeps its column at least N cells wide.

       .TS
       tab(@);
       lw(12) l.
       key@value
       .TE

        key            value

   [1mDefinitions with a narrow indent[0m
       A width on .TP sets the prevailing indent for the items that follow.

       .TP 4
       .B a
       first
       .TP
       .B b
       second

       [1ma[0m   first

       [1mb[0m   second

   [1mNumbered list and nested block[0m
       .IP 1. 4
       Write the page.
       .IP 2.
       Check it:
       .RS
       .nf
       roffsub \-c page.1
       .fi
       .RE

       1.  Write the page.

       2.  Check it:
           roffsub -c page.1

   [1mEmphasis[0m
       .BR roffsub (1)
       reads \fIfile\fR with \fB\-w\fR \fIwidth\fR.

       [1mroffsub[0m(1) reads [4mfile[0m with [1m-w[0m [4mwidth[0m.

[1mSEE ALSO[0m
       [1mroffsub[0m(1), [1mman[0m(7), [1mtbl[0m(1)
